    {
     "type": "note", "title": "21–99: the unit comes first",
     "md": "German says the **unit first**, then **und**, then the ten — like old English *four-and-twenty*:\n\n- 21 = {de:einundzwanzig} (*one-and-twenty*)\n- 45 = {de:fünfundvierzig} (*five-and-forty*)\n- 98 = {de:achtundneunzig}\n\nIt's written as **one word**. Careful when you hear a number: *fünfundvierzig* starts with the 5, but you write **45**.\n\n*eins* → **ein**undzwanzig. *sechs* and *sieben* stay complete here: {de:sechsundzwanzig}, {de:siebenundzwanzig}."
    },
